{"task_id": "HumanEval/31", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef is_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if a given integer n is a prime number.\n    \n    Args:\n        n: The integer to check.\n        \n    Returns:\n        True if n is prime, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        return False\n    if n <= 3:  # 2 and 3 are prime\n        return True\n    if n % 2 == 0 or n % 3 == 0:\n        return False\n    \n    # Check divisors from 5 up to sqrt(n)\n    # in steps of 6 (all primes > 3 are of the form 6k \u00b1 1)\n    i = 5\n    while i * i <= n:\n        if n % i == 0 or n % (i + 2) == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 6\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/31", "solution": "```python\nimport math\nimport os\nimport redis\nimport json\nimport logging\nimport sys\nfrom flask import Flask, request, jsonify\n\n# --- Configuration ---\n# Set up logging\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format='%(asctime)s - %(levelname)s - %(message)s')\napp_logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n# Redis configuration from environment variables or defaults\nREDIS_HOST = os.getenv('REDIS_HOST', 'localhost')\nREDIS_PORT = int(os.getenv('REDIS_PORT', 6379))\nREDIS_DB = int(os.getenv('REDIS_DB', 0))\n\ncache = None\ntry:\n    cache = redis.StrictRedis(host=REDIS_HOST, port=REDIS_PORT, db=REDIS_DB, decode_responses=True)\n    # Test connection\n    cache.ping()\n    app_logger.info(f\"Connected to Redis at {REDIS_HOST}:{REDIS_PORT}/{REDIS_DB}\")\nexcept redis.exceptions.ConnectionError as e:\n    app_logger.warning(f\"Could not connect to Redis: {e}. Running without caching.\")\n    cache = None # Ensure cache is None if connection fails\n\n# Maximum number allowed for primality test, aligning with performance expectations.\n# The SRS specifies up to 10^15 for efficient processing and 10^12 within 100ms.\n# Setting a hard upper limit to prevent Denial of Service (DoS) attacks with excessively large inputs.\nMAX_ALLOWED_NUMBER = 10**15\n\n# --- Core Primality Test Logic ---\ndef is_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given integer n is a prime number.\n    Optimized trial division algorithm.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Prime numbers are defined as natural numbers greater than 1.\n    if n <= 1:\n        return False\n    if n <= 3:  # 2 and 3 are prime numbers\n        return True\n    \n    # Check if n is divisible by 2 or 3\n    if n % 2 == 0 or n % 3 == 0:\n        return False\n    \n    # Check for divisors from 5 onwards using a step of 6.\n    # All primes greater than 3 can be expressed in the form 6k \u00b1 1.\n    i = 5\n    while i * i <= n:\n        if n % i == 0 or n % (i + 2) == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 6\n            \n    return True\n\n# --- Caching Layer for Primality Test ---\ndef cached_is_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if n is prime, utilizing a Redis cache if available.\n    \"\"\"\n    if cache:\n        cache_key = f\"prime:{n}\"\n        cached_result = cache.get(cache_key)\n        if cached_result is not None:\n            app_logger.debug(f\"Cache hit for {n}\")\n            return json.loads(cached_result)\n    \n    app_logger.debug(f\"Cache miss for {n}, calculating primality.\")\n    result = is_prime(n)\n    \n    if cache:\n        # Cache results indefinitely as primality is immutable\n        cache.set(cache_key, json.dumps(result))\n    \n    return result\n\n# --- Custom Exception for Input Validation ---\nclass InvalidInputError(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, message, status_code=400):\n        super().__init__(message)\n        self.message = message\n        self.status_code = status_code\n\n# --- Input Validation Module (Helper Functions) ---\ndef parse_and_validate_number(value_str, param_name=\"number\"):\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a string to an integer and validates its range for primality testing.\n    Raises InvalidInputError if validation fails.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        # Attempt to convert the input string to an integer\n        num = int(value_str)\n    except ValueError:\n        # If conversion fails, it's not a valid integer string.\n        raise InvalidInputError(f\"Invalid input. '{param_name}' must be an integer.\", 400)\n    except OverflowError:\n        # Python integers support arbitrary precision, so OverflowError is rare during conversion itself.\n        # This typically indicates the number is excessively large to be represented in fixed-size types,\n        # but in Python, it's more about practical processing limits.\n        raise InvalidInputError(f\"Input '{param_name}' is too large to safely process.\", 422)\n\n    # Prime numbers are strictly greater than 1.\n    # Inputs less than 1 are not candidates for primality.\n    if num < 1:\n        raise InvalidInputError(f\"Input '{param_name}' must be a positive integer greater than 0.\", 400)\n    \n    # Check if the number exceeds the defined maximum for efficient processing.\n    if num > MAX_ALLOWED_NUMBER:\n        raise InvalidInputError(\n            f\"Input '{param_name}' is out of acceptable range for efficient processing. \"\n            f\"Maximum allowed: {MAX_ALLOWED_NUMBER}.\", 422\n        )\n    \n    return num\n\n# --- API Error Handlers ---\n@app.errorhandler(InvalidInputError)\ndef handle_invalid_input_error(e):\n    \"\"\"Handles custom InvalidInputError, returning a JSON error response.\"\"\"\n    app_logger.warning(f\"Invalid input error: {e.status_code} - {e.message}\")\n    return jsonify({\"error\": e.message}), e.status_code\n\n@app.errorhandler(404)\ndef resource_not_found(e):\n    \"\"\"Handles 404 Not Found errors.\"\"\"\n    return jsonify({\"error\": \"Resource not found.\"}), 404\n\n@app.errorhandler(Exception)\ndef handle_internal_error(e):\n    \"\"\"\n    Handles all unhandled exceptions (internal server errors).\n    PRIME-NFR-004: In case of an internal server error, the service shall return\n    an HTTP 500 error code with a generic error message and log the details.\n    \"\"\"\n    app_logger.exception(\"An unhandled internal server error occurred.\")\n    return jsonify({\"error\": \"An internal server error occurred. Please try again later.\"}), 500\n\n# --- API Endpoints ---\n\n@app.route('/is_prime/<number_str>', methods=['GET'])\ndef check_single_primality_get(number_str):\n    \"\"\"\n    PRIME-REQ-001: Check Primality of a Single Number via GET request.\n    Input: An integer `n` as a path parameter.\n    Output: JSON object with the number and its primality status.\n    \"\"\"\n    num = parse_and_validate_number(number_str)\n    is_it_prime = cached_is_prime(num)\n    return jsonify({\"number\": num, \"is_prime\": is_it_prime}), 200\n\n@app.route('/is_prime', methods=['POST'])\ndef check_single_primality_post():\n    \"\"\"\n    PRIME-REQ-001: Check Primality of a Single Number via POST request.\n    Input: JSON object `{\"number\": N}` in the request body.\n    Output: JSON object with the number and its primality status.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not request.is_json:\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Request must be JSON.\", 400)\n    \n    data = request.get_json()\n    if not isinstance(data, dict) or 'number' not in data:\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Missing 'number' in request body or malformed JSON.\", 400)\n    \n    number_input = data['number']\n    \n    num = parse_and_validate_number(number_input, param_name=\"number\")\n    is_it_prime = cached_is_prime(num)\n    return jsonify({\"number\": num, \"is_prime\": is_it_prime}), 200\n\n@app.route('/is_prime/bulk', methods=['POST'])\ndef check_bulk_primality():\n    \"\"\"\n    PRIME-REQ-003: Bulk Primality Check.\n    Input: JSON array of integers `{\"numbers\": [N1, N2, ...]}` in the request body.\n    Output: JSON array of objects, each with a number and its primality status.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not request.is_json:\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Request must be JSON.\", 400)\n    \n    data = request.get_json()\n    if not isinstance(data, dict) or 'numbers' not in data:\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Missing 'numbers' array in request body or malformed JSON.\", 400)\n    \n    if not isinstance(data['numbers'], list):\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"'numbers' must be a JSON array of integers.\", 400)\n    \n    results = []\n    # Process each number in the bulk request\n    for i, number_input in enumerate(data['numbers']):\n        # If any item in the bulk request is invalid, the entire request fails as\n        # per SRS pre-condition for PRIME-REQ-003 (\"The input is a valid JSON array of integers\").\n        num = parse_and_validate_number(number_input, param_name=f\"numbers[{i}]\")\n        is_it_prime = cached_is_prime(num)\n        results.append({\"number\": num, \"is_prime\": is_it_prime})\n            \n    return jsonify(results), 200\n\n# --- Entry Point ---\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    # When running with 'flask run' or Gunicorn, this block is typically not executed.\n    # It's useful for local development and simple testing.\n    # For production, a WSGI server like Gunicorn should be used:\n    # e.g., gunicorn -w 4 -b 0.0.0.0:5000 app:app\n    app_logger.info(\"Starting Flask application in debug mode for development...\")\n    app.run(debug=True, host='0.0.0.0', port=5000)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "import math\n\n# Constants as per SRS/SDD\nTOLERANCE: float = 1e-6\nMAX_ITERATIONS: int = 1000\nINITIAL_SEARCH_RANGE: float = 1.0\nMAX_SEARCH_ATTEMPTS: int = 100\n\ndef poly(xs: list, x: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates a polynomial P(x) = xs[0] + xs[1]*x + ... + xs[n-1]*x^(n-1)\n    at a given point x.\n    \"\"\"\n    return sum([coeff * math.pow(x, i) for i, coeff in enumerate(xs)])\n\ndef find_zero(xs: list) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds a single real root for a polynomial defined by its coefficients xs.\n    Utilizes an initial bracketing search followed by the Bisection Method.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 3.1.2.2 Functional Process: Initial Bracketing Interval Search\n    low: float = -INITIAL_SEARCH_RANGE\n    high: float = INITIAL_SEARCH_RANGE\n    step: float = INITIAL_SEARCH_RANGE\n\n    for _ in range(MAX_SEARCH_ATTEMPTS):\n        p_low: float = poly(xs, low)\n        p_high: float = poly(xs, high)\n\n        if abs(p_low) < TOLERANCE:\n            return low\n        if abs(p_high) < TOLERANCE:\n            return high\n\n        if p_low * p_high < 0:\n            # A valid bracketing interval [low, high] has been found\n            break\n        else:\n            # Expand the search range\n            low -= step\n            high += step\n            step *= 2.0\n    \n    # As per SRS, a bracketing interval or a direct root will always be found\n    # within MAX_SEARCH_ATTEMPTS for valid inputs.\n    # Thus, 'low' and 'high' at this point define a valid bracket.\n\n    # 3.1.2.3 Functional Process: Bisection Iteration\n    mid: float = (low + high) / 2.0 # Initialize mid, will be updated in loop\n    for _ in range(MAX_ITERATIONS):\n        mid = (low + high) / 2.0\n        p_mid: float = poly(xs, mid)\n\n        if abs(p_mid) < TOLERANCE:\n            return mid\n        \n        # Determine which half of the interval contains the root\n        if poly(xs, low) * p_mid < 0:\n            # Root is in the lower half [low, mid]\n            high = mid\n        else:\n            # Root is in the upper half [mid, high]\n            low = mid\n    \n    # If MAX_ITERATIONS is reached, return the current mid value as the best approximation\n    return mid"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\n\ndef poly(xs, x):\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates a polynomial with coefficients `xs` at point `x`.\n    xs[0] + xs[1]*x + xs[2]*x^2 + ...\n    This function is provided as a placeholder based on the SRS, as it's\n    assumed to be pre-existing and correctly implemented.\n    \"\"\"\n    res = 0.0\n    for i, coeff in enumerate(xs):\n        res += coeff * math.pow(x, i)\n    return res\n\n\ndef find_zero(xs: list) -> float:\n    # --- Constants for precision and iteration limits (from Design 3.5) ---\n    INTERVAL_TOLERANCE = 1e-7\n    FUNCTION_TOLERANCE = 1e-9\n    MAX_BISECTION_ITERATIONS = 1000\n    \n    # Constants for initial bracket search (from Design 3.3)\n    INITIAL_SEARCH_RADIUS = 1.0\n    SEARCH_EXPANSION_FACTOR = 2.0\n    MAX_SEARCH_MAGNITUDE = 1e15 \n\n    # --- NFR1: Input Validation (Design 3.1) ---\n    # NFR1.1: xs must not be None or Empty\n    if xs is None or len(xs) == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Coefficient list cannot be empty or None.\")\n\n    # NFR1.3: All Coefficients Zero\n    if all(coeff == 0 for coeff in xs):\n        return 0.0\n\n    # NFR1.2: xs must have an even number of elements (odd degree polynomial)\n    if len(xs) % 2 != 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Coefficient list must have an even number of elements (odd degree polynomial).\")\n\n    # --- Initial Search Interval Determination (Design 3.3) ---\n\n    # 1. Check x = 0 as a potential root\n    y_at_zero = poly(xs, 0.0)\n    if abs(y_at_zero) < FUNCTION_TOLERANCE:\n        return 0.0\n\n    # 2. Expanding Search Strategy to find an initial bracketing interval [a, b]\n    a = -INITIAL_SEARCH_RADIUS\n    b = INITIAL_SEARCH_RADIUS\n    \n    y_a = poly(xs, a)\n    y_b = poly(xs, b)\n\n    # Edge case: initial bounds are roots themselves\n    if abs(y_a) < FUNCTION_TOLERANCE: return a\n    if abs(y_b) < FUNCTION_TOLERANCE: return b\n\n    # Loop to expand the interval until a bracket is found or max magnitude is reached.\n    # For odd-degree polynomials, a bracket is guaranteed to exist.\n    while y_a * y_b >= 0: # Continue as long as signs are the same or one is zero\n        a *= SEARCH_EXPANSION_FACTOR\n        b *= SEARCH_EXPANSION_FACTOR\n\n        # Safeguard against excessively large search bounds\n        if abs(a) > MAX_SEARCH_MAGNITUDE or abs(b) > MAX_SEARCH_MAGNITUDE:\n            # According to Design 3.3, if a bracket is not found within these bounds,\n            # an exceptional case has occurred. For odd-degree polynomials, a root\n            # is guaranteed. Failure to find a bracket implies numerical instability\n            # or a root beyond practical float representation.\n            raise RuntimeError(\n                \"Failed to find a bracketing interval within search limits \"\n                \"for an odd-degree polynomial. Numerical limits or precision issues.\"\n            )\n        \n        y_a = poly(xs, a)\n        y_b = poly(xs, b)\n        \n        # Check if the newly expanded bounds are roots themselves\n        if abs(y_a) < FUNCTION_TOLERANCE: return a\n        if abs(y_b) < FUNCTION_TOLERANCE: return b\n    \n    # At this point, we have a valid bracket [a, b] where y_a * y_b < 0.\n\n    # --- Root-Finding Process (Bisection Method) (Design 3.4) ---\n    iteration_count = 0\n\n    while abs(b - a) > INTERVAL_TOLERANCE and iteration_count < MAX_BISECTION_ITERATIONS:\n        midpoint = (a + b) / 2.0\n        y_mid = poly(xs, midpoint)\n\n        if abs(y_mid) < FUNCTION_TOLERANCE:\n            return midpoint\n\n        # If y_a and y_mid have opposite signs, the root is in [a, midpoint]\n        if y_a * y_mid < 0:\n            b = midpoint\n            y_b = y_mid # Update y_b to the function value at the new 'b'\n        else: # y_a * y_mid >= 0, the root is in [midpoint, b]\n            a = midpoint\n            y_a = y_mid # Update y_a to the function value at the new 'a'\n        \n        iteration_count += 1\n    \n    # After the loop, return the midpoint of the final, narrowed interval as the root estimate.\n    return (a + b) / 2.0\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_third(l: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts elements within a list that reside at indices divisible by three,\n    while preserving the original positions of all other elements.\n\n    Args:\n        l: The input list. Elements within the list must be sortable.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list that is a modified copy of the input list, where only\n        elements at indices 0, 3, 6, and so on, have been sorted in\n        ascending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Step 1: Create a Working Copy\n    l_prime = list(l)\n\n    # Step 2: Identify and Extract Elements & Indices\n    elements_to_sort = []\n    indices_to_modify = []\n\n    for i, val in enumerate(l_prime):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            elements_to_sort.append(val)\n            indices_to_modify.append(i)\n\n    # Step 3: Sort Extracted Elements\n    # This will raise a TypeError if elements are not sortable,\n    # which is expected behavior as per error handling considerations.\n    elements_to_sort.sort()\n\n    # Step 4: Replace Elements in l_prime\n    # Use zip to pair original indices with sorted elements, ensuring\n    # correct placement order (smallest sorted element goes to the first\n    # identified index, etc.)\n    for index_to_replace, sorted_val in zip(indices_to_modify, elements_to_sort):\n        l_prime[index_to_replace] = sorted_val\n\n    # Step 5: Return Result\n    return l_prime\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_third(l: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts elements at indices divisible by three in a list,\n    preserving the order of other elements, and returns a new list.\n\n    Args:\n        l: The input list.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list with elements at indices divisible by three sorted\n        in ascending order, and other elements preserved in their\n        original positions.\n    \"\"\"\n    values_to_sort = []\n    indices_to_replace = []\n\n    # First Pass - Extracting Elements and their original indices\n    for i, val in enumerate(l):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            values_to_sort.append(val)\n            indices_to_replace.append(i)\n\n    # Sorting Extracted Elements\n    values_to_sort.sort()\n\n    # Second Pass - Reconstructing the New List\n    # Create a shallow copy of the original list to preserve non-modified elements\n    l_prime = list(l)\n\n    # Place the sorted values back into their original divisible-by-three indices\n    for original_index, sorted_value in zip(indices_to_replace, values_to_sort):\n        l_prime[original_index] = sorted_value\n\n    return l_prime\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/34", "solution": "```python\ndef unique(l: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a new list containing all unique elements from the input list `l`,\n    sorted in ascending order.\n\n    This function processes the input list by first converting it to a set\n    to eliminate duplicates, then converts the set back to a list, and finally\n    sorts this list. The original input list remains unmodified.\n\n    Args:\n        l: The input list from which to extract unique and sorted elements.\n           Elements within 'l' must be hashable (for set conversion) and\n           comparable (for sorting).\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list with unique elements from `l`, sorted in ascending order.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If the input 'l' is not an iterable suitable for set conversion,\n                   or if elements within 'l' are not mutually comparable for sorting.\n\n    Example:\n        >>> unique([5, 3, 5, 2, 3, 3, 9, 0, 123])\n        [0, 2, 3, 5, 9, 123]\n        >>> unique([])\n        []\n        >>> unique(['b', 'a', 'c', 'a'])\n        ['a', 'b', 'c']\n    \"\"\"\n    return sorted(list(set(l)))\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/34", "solution": "```python\ndef unique(l: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Processes an input list, extracts its unique elements, and returns them in a sorted order.\n\n    Args:\n        l: The input list to process.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list containing unique elements from `l`, sorted in ascending order.\n        The original input list `l` is not modified.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Step 1: Deduplicate using a set.\n    # This automatically handles the removal of duplicate elements.\n    unique_set = set(l)\n\n    # Step 2: Convert the set back to a list and sort it.\n    # The sorted() function returns a new sorted list, fulfilling\n    # the requirements for output type, sorting, and immutability of input.\n    sorted_unique_list = sorted(list(unique_set))\n\n    return sorted_unique_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/35", "solution": "```python\ndef max_element(l: list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies and returns the maximum element from a given list.\n\n    This function iterates through the list to find the element with the\n    largest value. It handles various scenarios including positive/negative\n    numbers and single-element lists.\n\n    Args:\n        l (list): The input list from which to find the maximum element.\n                  Elements in the list are assumed to be comparable.\n\n    Returns:\n        The maximum element found in the list. The type of the output will\n        match the type of elements in the list (e.g., int, float, str).\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If the input list 'l' is empty.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> max_element([1, 2, 3])\n        3\n        >>> max_element([5, 3, -5, 2, -3, 3, 9, 0, 123, 1, -10])\n        123\n        >>> max_element([42])\n        42\n        >>> max_element([])\n        Traceback (most recent call last):\n            ...\n        ValueError: Input list cannot be empty\n        >>> max_element([-1, -5, -2])\n        -1\n        >>> max_element([3.14, 2.71, 1.618])\n        3.14\n        >>> max_element(['apple', 'banana', 'cherry'])\n        'cherry'\n    \"\"\"\n    if not l:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list cannot be empty\")\n\n    max_val = l[0]\n\n    # Iterate from the second element\n    for i in range(1, len(l)):\n        if l[i] > max_val:\n            max_val = l[i]\n\n    return max_val\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/35", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import Any\n\ndef max_element(l: list) -> Any:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the maximum element in the list.\n\n    Args:\n        l (list): A list of comparable elements.\n\n    Returns:\n        Any: The maximum element in the list.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If the input list is empty.\n        TypeError: If elements in the list are not comparable.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not l:  # Checks if the list is empty (len(l) == 0)\n        raise ValueError(\"max_element() arg is an empty sequence\")\n\n    max_val = l[0]  # Initialize max_val with the first element\n\n    # Iterate through the rest of the list, starting from the second element\n    for element in l[1:]:\n        if element > max_val:\n            max_val = element\n\n    return max_val\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/36", "solution": "```python\ndef fizz_buzz(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total number of times the digit '7' appears in positive integers\n    less than a given input 'n', provided those integers are divisible by either 11 or 13.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): An integer representing the upper bound (exclusive) for the numbers to be checked.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The total count of the digit '7' found in qualifying numbers.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> fizz_buzz(50)\n        0\n        >>> fizz_buzz(78)\n        2\n        >>> fizz_buzz(79)\n        3\n        >>> fizz_buzz(1)\n        0\n        >>> fizz_buzz(12)\n        0\n        >>> fizz_buzz(77)\n        0\n    \"\"\"\n    seven_count = 0  # REQ-FB-006: Initialize a running total\n\n    # REQ-FB-002: Iterate through all positive integers i such that 1 <= i < n\n    # The range(1, n) handles n <= 1 correctly by producing an empty sequence.\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        # REQ-FB-003: Check if i is divisible by 11 OR i is divisible by 13\n        if (i % 11 == 0) or (i % 13 == 0):\n            # REQ-FB-004: Convert i to its string representation\n            num_str = str(i)\n            # REQ-FB-005: Count the occurrences of the character '7' within the string\n            # REQ-FB-006: Add this count to the running total\n            seven_count += num_str.count('7')\n\n    # REQ-FB-007: Return the final running total\n    return seven_count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/36", "solution": "```python\ndef fizz_buzz(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total number of times the digit '7' appears in integers less than n\n    that are divisible by either 11 or 13.\n\n    Args:\n        n: A positive integer defining the upper bound (exclusive) for the search.\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the total count of the digit '7'.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sevens_count = 0\n\n    # Pre-condition: If n <= 1, no qualifying numbers exist in the range [1, n-1].\n    # Return 0 as per FR1.\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 0\n\n    # Iterate through each integer i from 1 up to (but not including) n.\n    # This covers the range 1 <= i < n as specified in FR1.\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        # Check if i is divisible by 11 or divisible by 13 as per FR1.\n        if i % 11 == 0 or i % 13 == 0:\n            # If the condition is met, convert the integer to its string representation\n            # to easily count occurrences of the digit '7'.\n            str_i = str(i)\n            # Count the occurrences of the character '7' within this string\n            # and add it to the running total.\n            total_sevens_count += str_i.count('7')\n\n    # After iterating through all i, return the accumulated total_sevens_count.\n    return total_sevens_count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/37", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_even(l: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts elements at even indices of a list in ascending order,\n    while preserving elements at odd indices in their original positions.\n\n    Args:\n        l: The input list.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list with even-indexed elements sorted and odd-indexed\n        elements preserved. The original list remains unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    # REQ-005: Handle empty list\n    if not l:\n        return []\n\n    # REQ-006: Handle single-element list\n    if len(l) == 1:\n        return list(l)  # Return a new list containing the element\n\n    even_elements = []\n    result_list = [None] * len(l)  # Initialize result list with placeholders\n\n    # Separate even-indexed elements and place odd-indexed elements directly\n    for i in range(len(l)):\n        if i % 2 == 0:  # Even index\n            even_elements.append(l[i])\n        else:  # Odd index (REQ-003)\n            result_list[i] = l[i]\n\n    # Sort the collected even-indexed elements (REQ-004)\n    # PERF-001: Python's Timsort gives O(K log K) where K is number of even elements\n    even_elements.sort()\n\n    # Re-populate the even indices in the result list with sorted elements\n    even_idx_tracker = 0\n    for i in range(len(l)):\n        if i % 2 == 0:  # Even index\n            result_list[i] = even_elements[even_idx_tracker]\n            even_idx_tracker += 1\n\n    # REQ-002: Return the new list\n    return result_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/37", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_even(l: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the odd indicies, while its values at the even indicies are equal\n    to the values of the even indicies of l, but sorted.\n\n    Args:\n        l (list): The input list. Elements at even indices must be comparable.\n\n    Returns:\n        list: A new list with odd-indexed elements preserved and even-indexed elements sorted.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> sort_even([1, 2, 3])\n        [1, 2, 3]\n        >>> sort_even([5, 6, 3, 4])\n        [3, 6, 5, 4]\n        >>> sort_even([])\n        []\n        >>> sort_even([10])\n        [10]\n        >>> sort_even([10, 20])\n        [10, 20]\n        >>> sort_even([4, 1, 2, 3, 0])\n        [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not l:\n        return []\n\n    # 1. Initialization: Create a copy of the input list for the result\n    result_list = list(l)\n    even_values = []\n\n    # 2. Extract Even-Indexed Values:\n    # Iterate through the original list to collect values at even indices\n    for i in range(len(l)):\n        if i % 2 == 0:\n            even_values.append(l[i])\n\n    # 3. Sort Even-Indexed Values:\n    # Sort the collected even-indexed values in ascending order\n    even_values.sort()\n\n    # 4. Reconstruct Output List:\n    # Place the sorted even values back into their original even index positions\n    even_idx_counter = 0\n    for i in range(len(l)):\n        if i % 2 == 0:\n            # Place the next sorted even value into the current even index\n            result_list[i] = even_values[even_idx_counter]\n            even_idx_counter += 1\n        # Elements at odd indices are already correct in result_list due to the initial copy\n\n    # 5. Return:\n    return result_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/38", "solution": "```python\ndef decode_cyclic(encoded_string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Decodes a string that has been cyclically shifted.\n\n    This function reverses the encoding performed by a hypothetical `encode_cyclic`\n    function. It processes the input string by dividing it into groups of three\n    characters. For each group of exactly three characters, it applies a cyclic\n    right shift (moving the last character to the front). Groups with fewer\n    than three characters are left unchanged. Finally, all processed groups\n    are concatenated to form the original decoded string.\n\n    Args:\n        encoded_string: The string to be decoded, which has been previously\n                        encoded using a cyclic left shift on groups of three.\n\n    Returns:\n        The decoded string, representing the original string before encoding.\n        Returns an empty string if the input `encoded_string` is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not encoded_string:\n        return \"\"\n\n    # 2. Grouping Module\n    list_of_groups = []\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(encoded_string):\n        group = encoded_string[i : i + 3]  # Slice up to 3 characters\n        list_of_groups.append(group)\n        i += 3\n\n    # 3. Transformation Module\n    list_of_processed_groups = []\n    for group in list_of_groups:\n        if len(group) == 3:\n            # Perform Cyclic Shift Right: last char to front\n            # Example: \"bca\" -> 'a' + 'bc' -> \"abc\"\n            shifted_group = group[2] + group[0] + group[1]\n            list_of_processed_groups.append(shifted_group)\n        else:\n            # Groups with fewer than 3 characters remain unchanged\n            list_of_processed_groups.append(group)\n\n    # 4. Concatenation Module\n    decoded_string = \"\".join(list_of_processed_groups)\n    return decoded_string\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/38", "solution": "```python\ndef decode_cyclic(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Decodes a cyclically encoded string.\n\n    This function reverses the cyclic encoding performed by a corresponding\n    encode_cyclic function. It processes the input string in groups of three\n    characters. For each group 'ch1ch2ch3', it transforms it back to\n    'ch3ch1ch2'. Any remaining characters at the end of the string (groups of\n    one or two characters) are left unchanged.\n\n    Args:\n        s: The cyclically encoded string.\n\n    Returns:\n        The original, decoded string.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not s:\n        return \"\"\n\n    decoded_parts = []\n    n = len(s)\n    i = 0\n\n    while i < n:\n        chunk = s[i : i + 3]\n        if len(chunk) == 3:\n            # If the chunk is 'bca' (ch1, ch2, ch3), it was originally 'abc'.\n            # So, 'a' is ch3, 'b' is ch1, 'c' is ch2.\n            # We want to reconstruct ch3 + ch1 + ch2.\n            ch1 = chunk[0]\n            ch2 = chunk[1]\n            ch3 = chunk[2]\n            decoded_parts.append(ch3 + ch1 + ch2)\n        else:\n            # Chunks of length 1 or 2 were not encoded, so append them directly.\n            decoded_parts.append(chunk)\n        i += 3\n\n    return \"\".join(decoded_parts)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/39", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef is_prime(num: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given integer is a prime number using trial division.\n\n    A prime number is a natural number greater than 1 that has no positive\n    divisors other than 1 and itself.\n\n    Args:\n        num (int): The integer to check for primality.\n\n    Returns:\n        bool: True if num is prime, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if num < 2:\n        return False\n    if num == 2:\n        return True\n    if num % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n    \n    # Check for odd divisors from 3 up to the square root of num\n    # We can skip even divisors since we already handled num % 2 == 0\n    limit = int(math.isqrt(num)) + 1 # isqrt is new in Python 3.8, safer to use int(sqrt()) for compatibility or just math.isqrt\n    # For older Python versions (<3.8), use: limit = int(math.sqrt(num)) + 1\n    \n    for divisor in range(3, limit, 2):\n        if num % divisor == 0:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef prime_fib(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies the n-th number that is both a Fibonacci number and a prime number.\n\n    The sequence starts with 2 (which is F(3)).\n    Fibonacci sequence: 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, ...\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The position of the desired prime Fibonacci number (n >= 1).\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The n-th Fibonacci number that is also prime.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If n is less than 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be a positive integer (n >= 1).\")\n\n    count = 0\n    a, b = 0, 1  # Initialize first two Fibonacci numbers (F_0, F_1)\n\n    while True:\n        a, b = b, a + b  # Generate the next Fibonacci number. 'a' now holds F_k\n\n        # Only check numbers greater than 1 as per prime definition\n        # is_prime(0) and is_prime(1) will correctly return False\n        if is_prime(a):\n            count += 1\n            if count == n:\n                return a\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/39", "solution": "```python\ndef is_prime(num: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if a given integer is a prime number using an optimized trial division algorithm.\n\n    Args:\n        num: An integer to check for primality.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if num is prime, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if num <= 1:\n        return False\n    if num <= 3:\n        return True\n    if num % 2 == 0 or num % 3 == 0:\n        return False\n    i = 5\n    while i * i <= num:\n        if num % i == 0 or num % (i + 2) == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 6\n    return True\n\ndef prime_fib(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    prime_fib returns n-th number that is a Fibonacci number and it's also prime.\n    >>> prime_fib(1)\n    2\n    >>> prime_fib(2)\n    3\n    >>> prime_fib(3)\n    5\n    >>> prime_fib(4)\n    13\n    >>> prime_fib(5)\n    89\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input n must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    count = 0  # Number of prime Fibonacci numbers found\n    a, b = 1, 1  # Initialize Fibonacci sequence (F(1), F(2))\n\n    while True:\n        current_fib = a + b  # Calculate the next Fibonacci number (starts with F(3)=2)\n        a = b\n        b = current_fib\n\n        if is_prime(current_fib):\n            count += 1\n            if count == n:\n                return current_fib\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/40", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef triples_sum_to_zero(l: List[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if there exist three distinct elements (at distinct indices) in the list that sum to zero.\n\n    Args:\n        l: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if there exist i, j, k such that 0 <= i < len(l), 0 <= j < len(l),\n        0 <= k < len(l), i \u2260 j, j \u2260 k, i \u2260 k, and l[i] + l[j] + l[k] == 0.\n        False otherwise.\n\n    Examples:\n        triples_sum_to_zero([1, 3, 5, 0]) == False\n        triples_sum_to_zero([1, 3, -2, 1]) == True  (e.g., l[0]=1, l[2]=-2, l[3]=1 sums to 0)\n        triples_sum_to_zero([1, 2, 3, 7]) == False\n        triples_sum_to_zero([2, 4, -5, 3, 9, 7]) == True (e.g., l[0]=2, l[2]=-5, l[3]=3 sums to 0)\n        triples_sum_to_zero([1]) == False\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(l)\n\n    # If the list has fewer than 3 elements, it's impossible to form a triplet.\n    if n < 3:\n        return False\n\n    # Create a sorted copy of the list.\n    # Sorting is essential for the two-pointer approach and also ensures the original list 'l'\n    # is not modified, as required by the post-conditions.\n    l_sorted = sorted(l)\n\n    # Iterate through each element to fix the first number of the triplet.\n    # The loop goes up to n - 2 because we need at least two more elements\n    # to form a triplet (handled by the 'left' and 'right' pointers).\n    for i in range(n - 2):\n        # We are looking for two numbers that sum to 'target_two_sum', where\n        # l_sorted[i] + l_sorted[left] + l_sorted[right] == 0\n        # implies l_sorted[left] + l_sorted[right] == -l_sorted[i].\n        target_two_sum = -l_sorted[i]\n\n        # Initialize two pointers: 'left' starts just after 'i', and 'right' at the end of the list.\n        left = i + 1\n        right = n - 1\n\n        # Use the two-pointer technique to find two elements that sum to 'target_two_sum'.\n        # The pointers move inwards until they cross each other.\n        while left < right:\n            current_two_sum = l_sorted[left] + l_sorted[right]\n\n            if current_two_sum == target_two_sum:\n                # A triplet (l_sorted[i], l_sorted[left], l_sorted[right]) that sums to zero has been found.\n                # Since 'i', 'left', and 'right' are distinct indices in the sorted array,\n                # they correspond to elements that originated from distinct indices in the original list.\n                # We only need to find if *any* such triplet exists, so we can return True immediately.\n                return True\n            elif current_two_sum < target_two_sum:\n                # The sum of the two pointers is too small. To increase the sum,\n                # move the 'left' pointer to the right to consider a larger value.\n                left += 1\n            else:  # current_two_sum > target_two_sum\n                # The sum of the two pointers is too large. To decrease the sum,\n                # move the 'right' pointer to the left to consider a smaller value.\n                right -= 1\n\n    # If the loops complete without finding any triplet that sums to zero, return False.\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/40", "solution": "```python\ndef triples_sum_to_zero(l: list[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if a given list of integers contains three distinct elements that sum to zero.\n\n    Args:\n        l: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if there exist three distinct indices i, j, k in the list l\n        such that l[i] + l[j] + l[k] == 0; False otherwise.\n        Returns False if the list contains fewer than three elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(l)\n\n    # REQ-FUN-005: Return False if the input list has fewer than three elements.\n    if n < 3:\n        return False\n\n    # Create a sorted copy of the input list.\n    # This takes O(N log N) time and O(N) space.\n    sorted_l = sorted(l)\n\n    # Iterate through the sorted list using an outer loop for the first element.\n    # The first element's index 'i' can go up to n-3 because we need at least two\n    # more elements (at 'left' and 'right' pointers).\n    # The overall time complexity will be O(N^2) due to the nested loops,\n    # satisfying NFR-PER-001.\n    for i in range(n - 2):\n        # Optimization: Skip duplicate values for the first element.\n        # This prevents redundant calculations for triplets that would yield\n        # the same value for the first element, while still ensuring distinct\n        # indices for the chosen triplet members (i, left, right).\n        if i > 0 and sorted_l[i] == sorted_l[i - 1]:\n            continue\n\n        # Define the target sum for the remaining two elements (left and right)\n        # such that sorted_l[i] + sorted_l[left] + sorted_l[right] == 0.\n        target_sum_for_pair = -sorted_l[i]\n\n        # Initialize two pointers for the sub-array after sorted_l[i].\n        left = i + 1\n        right = n - 1\n\n        # Use the two-pointer technique to find two elements that sum to target_sum_for_pair.\n        while left < right:\n            current_pair_sum = sorted_l[left] + sorted_l[right]\n\n            if current_pair_sum == target_sum_for_pair:\n                # REQ-FUN-003: A triplet (sorted_l[i], sorted_l[left], sorted_l[right])\n                # summing to zero has been found. The indices i, left, and right are\n                # guaranteed to be distinct (i < left < right).\n                return True\n            elif current_pair_sum < target_sum_for_pair:\n                # The sum is too small; increment the 'left' pointer to try a larger value.\n                left += 1\n            else:  # current_pair_sum > target_sum_for_pair\n                # The sum is too large; decrement the 'right' pointer to try a smaller value.\n                right -= 1\n\n    # REQ-FUN-004: If the loops complete without finding any triplet that sums to zero, return False.\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/41", "solution": "```python\ndef car_race_collision(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of collisions between two sets of 'n' cars\n    moving in opposite directions on an infinite line.\n\n    Each of the 'n' Left-to-Right (LTR) cars will collide with each\n    of the 'n' Right-to-Left (RTL) cars, as cars pass through each other\n    without affecting trajectory.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The number of cars moving left-to-right, and\n                 the number of cars moving right-to-left.\n                 Must be a non-negative integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The total number of collisions.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If 'n' is not an integer.\n        ValueError: If 'n' is a negative integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    # Based on the rule that cars pass through each other,\n    # each of the 'n' LTR cars will collide with each of the 'n' RTL cars.\n    # Therefore, the total number of collisions is n * n.\n    return n * n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/41", "solution": "```python\ndef car_race_collision(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total number of collisions between two sets of 'n' cars\n    (n L-cars moving left-to-right and n R-cars moving right-to-left)\n    on an infinitely long straight road. All cars move at the same speed,\n    are infinitely sturdy, and continue their trajectory after a collision.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The number of cars in each set (non-negative integer).\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The total number of collisions.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If 'n' is a negative integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    # Each of the 'n' L-cars will collide with each of the 'n' R-cars.\n    # The total number of collisions is simply n * n.\n    total_collisions = n * n\n\n    return total_collisions\n```"}
