{"task_id": "HumanEval/57", "solution": "```python\ndef monotonic(l: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if a given list of numbers is monotonically increasing or monotonically decreasing.\n\n    A list is monotonic if it is either entirely non-decreasing (each element is\n    greater than or equal to the previous) or entirely non-increasing (each\n    element is less than or equal to the previous).\n\n    Args:\n        l: The input list of comparable elements (e.g., numbers).\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the list is monotonic (non-decreasing or non-increasing), False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(l) <= 1:\n        # FR-2: Empty list is monotonic.\n        # FR-3: Single-element list is monotonic.\n        return True\n\n    increasing_trend_found = False\n    decreasing_trend_found = False\n\n    for i in range(1, len(l)):\n        current_element = l[i]\n        previous_element = l[i-1]\n\n        if current_element > previous_element:\n            increasing_trend_found = True\n        elif current_element < previous_element:\n            decreasing_trend_found = True\n\n        # FR-1: If both increasing and decreasing trends are found,\n        # the list is not monotonic.\n        if increasing_trend_found and decreasing_trend_found:\n            return False\n\n    # If the loop completes, it means only one type of trend (or no trend, i.e., all equal)\n    # was observed, satisfying the monotonic condition.\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/57", "solution": "```python\ndef monotonic(l: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a list is monotonically increasing or decreasing.\n\n    A list is monotonic if its elements are consistently non-decreasing\n    (each element is greater than or equal to the previous one) OR\n    consistently non-increasing (each element is less than or equal\n    to the previous one).\n\n    Empty lists and single-element lists are considered monotonic.\n\n    Args:\n        l: The input list of comparable elements.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the list is monotonic (increasing or decreasing), False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(l) <= 1:\n        return True\n\n    is_increasing = True\n    is_decreasing = True\n\n    for i in range(1, len(l)):\n        if l[i] < l[i - 1]:\n            is_increasing = False\n        if l[i] > l[i - 1]:\n            is_decreasing = False\n\n        # Early exit optimization: if neither trend is maintained, it's not monotonic\n        if not is_increasing and not is_decreasing:\n            return False\n\n    return is_increasing or is_decreasing\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/58", "solution": "```python\ndef common(l1: list, l2: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds unique, sorted common elements between two input lists.\n\n    Args:\n        l1: The first input list.\n        l2: The second input list.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list containing unique common elements, sorted in ascending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Convert to Sets: Convert both input lists to sets to efficiently handle\n    #    duplicates within each list and prepare for intersection.\n    set1 = set(l1)\n    set2 = set(l2)\n\n    # 2. Find Intersection: Determine the common elements by performing a set intersection.\n    #    The result will inherently contain only unique elements.\n    common_elements_set = set1.intersection(set2)\n\n    # 3. Convert to List: Convert the set of unique common elements back into a list\n    #    as per the output requirement.\n    common_elements_list = list(common_elements_set)\n\n    # 4. Sort List: Sort the list of common elements in ascending order.\n    common_elements_list.sort()\n\n    # 5. Return Sorted List: Return the final list.\n    return common_elements_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/58", "solution": "```python\ndef common(l1: list, l2: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies and returns unique common elements present in two input lists,\n    sorted in ascending order.\n\n    Args:\n        l1: The first input list. Expected to contain comparable elements.\n        l2: The second input list. Expected to contain comparable elements.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of unique common elements found in both l1 and l2,\n        sorted in ascending order. An empty list is returned if no\n        common elements are found.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Convert input lists to sets for efficient unique element handling and intersection\n    set1 = set(l1)\n    set2 = set(l2)\n\n    # Find the intersection of the two sets\n    # This results in a set of unique common elements\n    common_elements_set = set1.intersection(set2)\n\n    # Convert the resulting set back to a list\n    common_elements_list = list(common_elements_set)\n\n    # Sort the list of common elements in ascending order\n    common_elements_list.sort()\n\n    # Return the sorted list\n    return common_elements_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/59", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_prime_factor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the largest prime factor of a given positive composite integer.\n\n    Args:\n        n: A positive composite integer (n > 1 and n is not prime).\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the largest prime factor of n.\n    \"\"\"\n    largest_factor = 1\n\n    # Handle factor 2\n    # Divide n by 2 until it's odd, updating largest_factor if 2 is a factor.\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        largest_factor = 2\n        n //= 2\n\n    # Iterate for odd factors starting from 3\n    # We only need to check factors up to sqrt(n).\n    # The loop condition i * i <= n is equivalent to i <= sqrt(n)\n    # but avoids floating-point calculations and n is continuously reduced.\n    i = 3\n    while i * i <= n:\n        # While i divides n, update largest_factor and reduce n\n        while n % i == 0:\n            largest_factor = i\n            n //= i\n        # Increment i by 2 to check only odd numbers\n        i += 2\n\n    # After the loop, if n is still greater than 2, it means the remaining\n    # n is a prime number itself (and greater than any factor found so far).\n    # This prime number must be the largest prime factor.\n    if n > 2:\n        largest_factor = n\n\n    return largest_factor\n```"}
